    {
      "id": "SA-C28-svasti",
      "sequence": 1370,
      "title": "स्वस्ति (svasti) — well-being (may it go well)",
      "headword": "स्वस्ति",
      "romanization": "svasti",
      "gloss": "well-being (may it go well)",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:080ea33871d11667",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of this run: what did śubham mean, and what did idānīm mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: स्वस्ति",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "स्वस्ति (svasti) — \"well-being (may it go well)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "स्वस्ति (svasti) is the oldest farewell in this book, and you can take it apart with what you already have. Su- means \"well\" — it is the su- inside स्वागतम्, \"well-come\". Asti means \"is\". So स्वस्ति (svasti) is, exactly, it is well — said as a wish over someone leaving."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Add the little ending -ka and you have svastika, \"the mark of well-being\", a good-luck sign that was drawn on doorways for thousands of years before the twentieth century took it and made it unusable in the West. The word is not the thing; but the word means what it means."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five ways to leave: now, the day after tomorrow, a journey, a good wish, and well-being itself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
